From f07688330966e9cde0a3f4a6bbe3da7984f87710 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 09:08:25 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] net: udp: keep DHCP out of GRO

With fraglist GRO on by default (600-net-enable-fraglist-GRO-by-default),
UDP datagrams of one flow that arrive in the same NAPI poll are merged
even when no socket asked for GRO; with rx-udp-gro-forwarding on they
are merged the same way without fraglist. Before it has a lease, busybox
udhcpc reads DHCP replies from a packet socket, and packet sockets get
the merged skb as it is: one IP/UDP header, both DHCP messages behind
it, and a UDP checksum that holds for neither. udhcpc drops it ("bad UDP
checksum"), so a DHCP server or AP that delivers each reply twice, back
to back, makes DHCP fail.

Seen on an ath11k station: every OFFER arrived as two identical messages
merged into one 656-byte datagram and was ignored, 20 s, no lease. With
rx-gro-list off the same exchange finished in 0.1 s.

DHCP gains nothing from GRO; leave UDP ports 67 and 68 out of it before
either GRO mode is chosen.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
---
 net/ipv4/udp_offload.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

--- a/net/ipv4/udp_offload.c
+++ b/net/ipv4/udp_offload.c
@@ -806,6 +806,14 @@ struct sk_buff *udp_gro_receive(struct l
 		if (skb->encapsulation)
 			goto out;
 
+		/* DHCP clients, and some servers, receive through packet
+		 * sockets, which get a GRO packet unsegmented: two DHCP
+		 * messages behind one header, with a UDP checksum valid for
+		 * neither. DHCP gains nothing from GRO, so keep it out.
+		 */
+		if (uh->dest == htons(67) || uh->dest == htons(68))
+			goto out;
+
 		if (skb->dev->features & NETIF_F_GRO_FRAGLIST)
 			NAPI_GRO_CB(skb)->is_flist = sk ? !udp_test_bit(GRO_ENABLED, sk) : 1;
 
